fix_message_optimistic_reactivity.s:
Root cause: in both src/routes/app/chat/[id]/+page.svelte:138-191 (send()) and src/routes/app/rooms/[id]/+page.svelte:133-181 (send()), the optimistic message row is confirmed/failed by mutating a stale object reference held across an `await` boundary:
```js
if (!res?.ok) {
	if (row) row.err = true;
	return;
}
mark_first_send();
const { m } = await res.json();
if (row && m) {
	const found = messages.find((e) => e.cid === row.cid);
	if (found) {
		found.id = m.id;
		found.d = m.ts;
	}
}
```
`messages = $state(...)` is a deep-reactive array; `row` was pushed via `messages = [...messages, row]` (chat page: add_msg() at line 49-54; rooms page: inline at line 158) — a full reassignment of `messages`, which re-wraps the new array (and, on first read, its elements) in fresh Svelte 5 state proxies. Mutating `found.id = m.id` in place relies on `found` being the exact live proxy the template's `{#each messages as m}` is bound to; the template checks `class:opacity-60={m.id === '' && !m.err}` (chat page line 432, rooms page line 430). When this in-place mutation doesn't propagate (proxy identity lost across the reassignment + await gap — the class of bug Svelte 5 docs warn about for object mutation through async closures), the bubble never loses `opacity-60` and stays visually dark until a full page reload re-fetches real (non-empty-id) messages from +page.server.ts.

Fix: replace in-place mutation with immutable reassignment via `.map()`, which is guaranteed to trigger reactivity regardless of proxy identity, in a new shared helper so both pages route through one implementation instead of duplicating the bug-prone logic.

Create src/lib/chat_optimistic.ts (new file):
```ts
export function confirm_sent<T extends { cid?: string }>(
	messages: T[],
	cid: string,
	patch: Partial<T>
): T[] {
	return messages.map((m) => (m.cid === cid ? { ...m, ...patch } : m));
}

export function mark_failed<T extends { cid?: string; err?: boolean }>(messages: T[], cid: string): T[] {
	return messages.map((m) => (m.cid === cid ? { ...m, err: true } : m));
}
```

In src/routes/app/chat/[id]/+page.svelte: add `import { confirm_sent, mark_failed } from '$lib/chat_optimistic';` near the top imports (after the ws import line 5). Replace lines 178-190:
```js
		if (!res?.ok) {
			if (row) row.err = true;
			return;
		}
		mark_first_send();
		const { m } = await res.json();
		if (row && m) {
			const found = messages.find((e) => e.cid === row.cid);
			if (found) {
				found.id = m.id;
				found.d = m.ts;
			}
		}
```
with:
```js
		if (!res?.ok) {
			if (row?.cid) messages = mark_failed(messages, row.cid);
			return;
		}
		mark_first_send();
		const { m } = await res.json();
		if (row?.cid && m) messages = confirm_sent(messages, row.cid, { id: m.id, d: m.ts });
```

Apply the identical replacement in src/routes/app/rooms/[id]/+page.svelte lines 168-180 (same shape, same import to add after line 5). Note the rooms page's `row` type is `Row = Message & { cid?: string; err?: boolean; fl?: FileAttach }` (line 28) — `confirm_sent`/`mark_failed` are generic over `{ cid?: string }` so this typechecks without changes.

Do not touch the `retry` path (`if (retry) { retry.err = false; row = retry; }`) — `retry` is itself already a live element read out of `messages` via the button's closure (`onclick={() => send(m)}` where `m` is the current live `{#each}` binding), so it is already the correct proxy; only the async-confirm path after `await fetch` is affected.

Why `.map()` over `Object.assign`/re-find-then-mutate again: it works correctly regardless of Svelte 5's internal proxy-identity mechanics (no reliance on `.find()` returning a still-live reference after an await gap), it is the pattern the codebase already uses correctly elsewhere for reactive array updates (e.g. group.ts's spread-based updates), and it is one line, not a new abstraction.

Test: create src/lib/__tests__/chat_optimistic.test.ts:
```ts
