add_device_cookie_and_session_plumbing.s:
Goal: a brand-new visitor can start using rooms with zero signup — a lightweight "device account" is created for them automatically the moment they actually try to do something (join a room, send a message), not on every idle pageview. This step lays the plumbing (cookie, types, session flag, core helper functions); [[wire_lazy_account_creation_into_room_join_and_send]] wires it into the actual routes and the layout gate.

**Cookie**: `src/hooks.server.ts` currently (full file already read this session) sets `event.locals.x2_ws` (line 25) then reads the `session` cookie (line 27 onward). Insert a `device_id` cookie block between those two, so every request — authenticated or not — carries a stable per-browser id from its very first hit:
```ts
	event.locals.x2_ws = x2_ws ?? devFetcher();

	let device_id = event.cookies.get('device_id');
	if (!device_id) {
		device_id = crypto.randomUUID();
		event.cookies.set('device_id', device_id, {
			path: '/',
			httpOnly: true,
			maxAge: 60 * 60 * 24 * 400,
			sameSite: 'lax'
		});
	}
	event.locals.device_id = device_id;

	const session_id = event.cookies.get('session');
```
400 days, not a round "1 year" or longer: Chrome (and the spec) caps `Set-Cookie` `Max-Age` at 400 days and silently clamps anything higher — 400 is the actual ceiling, not an arbitrary round number.

**Types**: `src/app.d.ts`'s `Locals` interface (lines 21-31, full file already read) needs `device_id: string;` added alongside the existing `user`/`x2_ws`/`geo`/`bg` members, and `user`'s inline type (line 22) needs `is_device?: boolean` added: `user?: { id: string; username: string; picture?: string; email?: string; is_device?: boolean } | null;`.

`src/lib/types.ts`'s `User` interface (already read this session) has `o?: 'google' | 'local'; // provider` — widen to `o?: 'google' | 'local' | 'device'; // provider — 'device' means created implicitly, no password or google link yet`.

**Session flag**: `src/lib/server/session.ts` (full file already read, 47 lines). `SessionUser` (line 11) gets one more optional field:
```ts
export type SessionUser = { id: string; username: string; picture?: string; email?: string; v?: number; is_device?: boolean };
```
`encode_session` (lines 14-27) — add a `dv` (device) short key to the signed wire payload, matching this file's existing single/short-key convention (`u`,`n`,`p`,`m`,`v`,`e`):
```ts
export async function encode_session(secret: SecretVal, data: SessionUser): Promise<string> {
	const p = {
		u: data.id,
		n: data.username,
		p: data.picture,
		m: data.email,
		v: data.v ?? 0,
		dv: data.is_device ? 1 : 0,
		e: Date.now() + 604800000
	};
```
`decode_session` (lines 29-47) — return it:
```ts
		return { user: { id: p.u, username: p.n, picture: p.p, email: p.m, is_device: !!p.dv }, v: p.v ?? 0 };
```
Every EXISTING caller of `encode_session` (google/+server.ts, register/+server.ts, login/+server.ts) is unaffected — `is_device` is optional and they don't pass it, so it defaults falsy exactly as intended (a real google/password login is never a device-only account). Do not edit those three call sites in this step.

**`is_device_only` helper** — single source of truth for "does this user record still have no real credential," so every place that needs to know (session issuance, the upgrade-prompt UI in [[add_account_upgrade_prompt_ui]]) computes it the same way instead of each guessing independently. Add to `src/lib/server/user.ts` (full file already read, 112 lines), after `get_user` (line 46):
```ts
export function is_device_only(u: Pick<User, 'h' | 'o'>): boolean {
	return !u.h && u.o !== 'google';
}
```
True exactly when there's no password hash AND the account didn't originate from (or later link) Google — i.e. still purely device-bound.

**Core helpers** — create `src/lib/server/device.ts` (new file):
```ts
import type { QEnv, SecretVal } from './qdrant';
import { uuid_from } from './qdrant';
import { get_user, save_user, is_device_only, type User } from './user';
import { encode_session } from './session';
import { guard } from './rl';
import type { Cookies } from '@sveltejs/kit';

export async function get_or_create_device_user(env: QEnv, device_id: string): Promise<User & { id: string }> {
	const id = await uuid_from(device_id);
	const existing = await get_user(env, id);
	if (existing) return { ...existing, id };
	await save_user(env, device_id, undefined, undefined, 'device');
	return { ...(await get_user(env, id))!, id };
}

// Called at the top of the small set of write routes a brand-new, never-logged-in visitor can
// reach (send a message, create/join a room). Everywhere else in the app already requires a
// real session (reached only via the room-join flow this establishes, or a real login) — this
// is the single place a session gets minted with no credential behind it, so it's also the one
// place a rate limit keyed on something other than a uid belongs (see RL_DEVICE_CREATE below;
// every other limiter in this codebase is deliberately uid-only, per the comment in
// wrangler.jsonc — this is a narrow, justified exception because no uid exists yet).
export async function ensure_device_session(
	env: QEnv & { SECRET?: SecretVal },
	platform: App.Platform | undefined,
	locals: App.Locals,
	cookies: Cookies,
	get_client_address: () => string
): Promise<{ id: string; username: string } | null> {
	if (locals.user) return locals.user;
	const device_id = locals.device_id;
	if (!device_id) return null;
	await guard(platform, 'RL_DEVICE_CREATE', get_client_address());
	const u = await get_or_create_device_user(env, device_id);
	const session = await encode_session(env.SECRET, { id: u.id, username: u.u, is_device: is_device_only(u) });
	cookies.set('session', session, { path: '/', httpOnly: true, maxAge: 604800, sameSite: 'lax' });
	locals.user = { id: u.id, username: u.u };
	return locals.user;
}
```
`is_device_only(u)` (not a hardcoded `true`) is deliberate, not decorative: `device_id` cookies last 400 days but `session` cookies last only 7 — a visitor who linked a real password weeks ago can still have their *session* expire while their *device_id* cookie survives, land back on `ensure_device_session` with no active session, and get looked up via `get_or_create_device_user` — which now correctly finds their already-linked record and must report `is_device: false`, not `true`, or the upgrade banner would wrongly reappear for someone who already upgraded. `guard()` (rl.ts, already read this session) throws its own 429 on failure — no try/catch needed here, it propagates exactly like every other existing `guard()` call site in this codebase.

**Rate limit binding** — add to `wrangler.jsonc`'s `ratelimits` array (lines 95-100, exact current contents already read):
```jsonc
	"ratelimits": [
		{ "name": "RL_SEND", "namespace_id": "1001", "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 10 } },
		{ "name": "RL_UPLOAD", "namespace_id": "1002", "simple": { "limit": 10, "period": 60 } },
		{ "name": "RL_SEARCH", "namespace_id": "1003", "simple": { "limit": 10, "period": 60 } },
		{ "name": "RL_AI", "namespace_id": "1004", "simple": { "limit": 5, "period": 60 } },
		{ "name": "RL_DEVICE_CREATE", "namespace_id": "1005", "simple": { "limit": 5, "period": 60 } }
	],
```
5 per 60s, keyed on IP (the caller passes `get_client_address()`, i.e. `getClientAddress()`, as the limiter key — see [[wire_lazy_account_creation_into_room_join_and_send]]) — generous enough for a shared-NAT household to each get an account within a minute, tight enough to blunt a scripted mass-account-creation loop. `namespace_id` "1005" continues this file's existing sequential numbering; confirm no other binding already claims it before finalizing (grep `wrangler.jsonc` for `100` to be sure).

