wire_lazy_account_creation_into_room_joi.s:
Wires [[add_device_cookie_and_session_plumbing]]'s helpers into the actual places a brand-new visitor can trigger account creation, and — the part that's easy to miss — makes those places actually *reachable* while logged out, since today's layout gate bounces every unauthenticated request under `/app/*` straight to `/login` before any page (or its send/join button) ever renders.

**1. The layout gate.** `src/routes/+layout.server.ts` — by the time this step runs, [[redirect_home_to_rooms_when_authed]] has already landed, so the file currently reads:
```ts
export const load: LayoutServerLoad = async ({ locals, url }) => {
	const open = url.pathname === '/' || url.pathname === '/login';
	if (!locals.user && !open) throw redirect(302, '/login');
	if (locals.user && url.pathname === '/login') throw redirect(302, '/app');
	if (locals.user && url.pathname === '/') throw redirect(302, '/app/rooms');
	return { user: locals.user };
};
```
Widen `open` to also cover room-browsing/viewing, since those are the only `/app/*` surfaces a logged-out visitor should ever reach — everything else (chats, profile, people-matching) still requires a real session exactly as today:
```ts
export const load: LayoutServerLoad = async ({ locals, url }) => {
	const open =
		url.pathname === '/' ||
		url.pathname === '/login' ||
		url.pathname === '/app/rooms' ||
		url.pathname.startsWith('/app/rooms/');
	if (!locals.user && !open) throw redirect(302, '/login');
	if (locals.user && url.pathname === '/login') throw redirect(302, '/app');
	if (locals.user && url.pathname === '/') throw redirect(302, '/app/rooms');
	return { user: locals.user };
};
```

**2. Anonymous room preview.** `src/routes/app/rooms/[id]/+page.server.ts` (full file, 18 lines, already read) currently hard-401s anyone without a session:
```ts
export const load: PageServerLoad = async ({ params, locals }) => {
	if (!locals.user) throw error(401, 'auth');
	const g = await get_group(env, params.id);
	if (!g) throw error(404, 'no group');
	const messages = await get_group_messages(env, params.id);

	const ids = [...new Set([...messages.map((m) => m.f), ...g.members])];
	const names = await get_user_names(env, ids);
	const muted = await is_muted(env, locals.x2_ws, locals.user.id, params.id);
	return { g, messages, names, muted };
};
```
Change to a graceful anonymous preview — room info and member list, but not message history (a room's conversation content isn't shown to someone who hasn't even joined yet, device account or not):
```ts
export const load: PageServerLoad = async ({ params, locals }) => {
	const g = await get_group(env, params.id);
	if (!g) throw error(404, 'no group');
	const messages = locals.user ? await get_group_messages(env, params.id) : [];
	const ids = [...new Set([...messages.map((m) => m.f), ...g.members])];
	const names = await get_user_names(env, ids);
	const muted = locals.user ? await is_muted(env, locals.x2_ws, locals.user.id, params.id) : false;
	return { g, messages, names, muted };
};
```

**3. The join action.** `src/routes/api/groups/[id]/+server.ts` (full file, 50 lines, already read) — the room page's template already hides the compose form from non-members (`mine = $derived(!!me && g.members.includes(me))`, rooms/[id]/+page.svelte) and only exposes a "join" button, so joining is the one action an anonymous room visitor can actually take — this is the concrete, UI-reachable version of "as soon as the user tries... joining a group, it makes an account for them." Change its `POST` handler (lines 34-43):
```ts
export const POST: RequestHandler = async ({ params, request, locals, platform, cookies, getClientAddress }) => {
	const me = locals.user ?? (await ensure_device_session(env, platform, locals, cookies, getClientAddress));
	if (!me) throw error(401, 'auth');
	const b = (await request.json().catch(() => null)) as { action?: string };
	const g =
		b?.action === 'leave'
			? await leave_group(env, locals.x2_ws, params.id, me.id)
			: await join_group(env, locals.x2_ws, params.id, me.id);
	if (!g) throw error(400, b?.action === 'leave' ? 'owner cannot leave' : 'no group');
	return json({ g });
};
```
(add `import { ensure_device_session } from '$lib/server/device';` to this file's imports; `leave` is included in the same branch for simplicity even though a brand-new anonymous visitor can never actually be a member yet to leave — `leave_group` already fails closed for a non-member, so no extra guard is needed here).

Also apply the identical `ensure_device_session` treatment to `POST` in `src/routes/api/groups/+server.ts` (lines 20-41, already read) — creating a room is the other half of "joining a group" in spirit, and costs nothing extra to support the same way:
```ts
export const POST: RequestHandler = async ({ request, locals, platform, cookies, getClientAddress }) => {
	const me = locals.user ?? (await ensure_device_session(env, platform, locals, cookies, getClientAddress));
	if (!me) throw error(401, 'auth');
	await guard(platform, 'RL_SEARCH', me.id);
	const b = (await request.json().catch(() => null)) as {
		name?: string; description?: string; country?: string; state?: string; city?: string;
	};
	const name = b?.name?.trim();
	if (!name) throw error(400, 'name required');
	return json({ g: await save_group(env, locals.x2_ws, me.id, { name, description: b?.description, country: b?.country, state: b?.state, city: b?.city }) });
};
```

And `src/routes/api/send/+server.ts` (full file, already read) — the literal "as soon as the user tries chatting" trigger. Replace line 26 (`if (!locals.user) throw error(401, 'auth');`) and its handler signature (line 25) the same way:
```ts
export const POST: RequestHandler = async ({ request, locals, platform, cookies, getClientAddress }) => {
	const me = locals.user ?? (await ensure_device_session(env, platform, locals, cookies, getClientAddress));
	if (!me) throw error(401, 'auth');
	await guard(platform, 'RL_SEND', me.id);
```
and delete the file's original `const me = locals.user;` (line 44) — `me` is now established once at the top, already null-checked, and every later use of `me` in this file (both the group-send and 1:1-send branches) keeps working unchanged since it's the same shape (`{id, username}` has everything those branches read off `me.id`/`me.username`). Note there is currently no UI that lets a logged-out visitor reach `/app/chat/[id]` at all (that route stays behind the normal login gate — only rooms got an anonymous entry point in this pass), so this specific wiring is forward-compatible rather than immediately user-reachable; leave it in anyway since it's a two-line change and correctly future-proofs any later anonymous-1:1 entry point without needing to revisit this file again.

**4. Client-side: joining while anonymous must refresh the session state.** `src/routes/app/rooms/[id]/+page.svelte` (full file, already read) — `me` is derived from `$page.data.user?.id` (line 40, `let me = $derived($page.data.user?.id);`), which only changes when the page's load function re-runs. Today's `membership()` (lines 183-190) only swaps in the updated group (`g = (await res.json()).g;`) — after an anonymous join, the server has now set a *new* session cookie, but the client's `$page.data.user` is still stale (null) until something re-invalidates the load. Fix:
```js
	async function membership(action: 'join' | 'leave') {
		const res = await fetch(`/api/groups/${g.id}`, {
			method: 'POST',
			headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
			body: JSON.stringify({ action })
		});
		if (res.ok) {
			g = (await res.json()).g;
			if (!me) await invalidateAll();
		}
	}
```
add `import { invalidateAll } from '$app/navigation';` to the existing import block (near `import { goto } from '$app/navigation';`, line 2 — combine into one import statement from the same module). `invalidateAll()` re-runs `+layout.server.ts` and this page's own `+page.server.ts` load, which now see the freshly-set `session` cookie and populate `$page.data.user` for real — at which point `mine` (line 41, `$derived(!!me && g.members.includes(me))`) flips true and the compose form appears, with zero other template changes needed since everything already keys off `mine`/`me` reactively. Only call `invalidateAll()` when `!me` (was previously anonymous) — an already-logged-in user's join doesn't need a full reload, matching today's behavior exactly for that case.

**5. Cross-reference correction to an earlier step.** [[rebuild_home_as_room_search]] (already executed by the time this step runs) linked every room card and the primary CTA on the public home page straight to `/login` (`<a href="/login" ...>{g.name}</a>`) — now that `/app/rooms/{id}` itself is reachable while logged out, change those links to go there directly instead: `<a href="/app/rooms/{g.id}" ...>{g.name}</a>`, and the bottom CTA's `href="/login"` → `href="/app/rooms"`. A logged-out visitor now lands on the real room (or room list) and joins in place, triggering device-account creation exactly at the point of actual intent, instead of being routed through a signup wall the whole rest of this feature exists to remove. [[rewrite_site_copy]]'s copy for that CTA ("start free — join or create a room") stays accurate either way — update only the `href`, not the wording.

Test: extend `src/routes/__tests__/layout.server.test.ts` (created in [[redirect_home_to_rooms_when_authed]]) with cases confirming `/app/rooms` and `/app/rooms/abc123` are NOT redirected for a logged-out visitor, while `/app/chats` still is. Extend `src/routes/app/rooms/[id]/__tests__/page.server.test.ts` (create if absent) with: an anonymous load returns the group and names but an empty `messages` array and `muted: false`, without ever calling `is_muted`. Extend the room page's component test with: clicking join while logged out calls `invalidateAll` after a successful response; clicking join while already logged in does not. Extend `groups/[id]/__tests__/server.test.ts` and `groups/__tests__/server.test.ts` (create if absent, mirroring this plan's established `vi.hoisted`+`vi.mock` style) with: an anonymous POST with a valid `device_id` in locals succeeds and calls `join_group`/`save_group` with the newly-minted device uid; an anonymous POST with no `device_id` at all still 401s. Extend `send/__tests__/server.test.ts` identically. Watch all fail first, implement, then green. Run `pnpm test` and `pnpm check`.

add_account_credential_linking.s:
The trap this step exists to close: this codebase's local-auth system finds a user by *deriving* their id straight from their credential (`uuid_from(email)` for password login, `uuid_from(google_sub)` for Google login — both confirmed in `src/lib/server/user.ts` and `src/routes/google/+server.ts`, already read this session) rather than by looking them up through an index. A device account's id is `uuid_from(device_id)` — a third, unrelated string. Simply letting a logged-in device user "go through" the existing register/login/Google flows would compute a *different* id than the one their room history already lives under, silently forking them into two disconnected accounts instead of upgrading the one they have. Fixing this needs a real lookup-by-credential mechanism, not just permission to reuse the existing flows.

**1. Index email for lookup.** `email` (`m`) is not currently in `KEYWORD_KEYS` (`src/lib/server/qdrant.ts` line 99, already read: `'s', 't', 'r', 'c', 'f', 'co', 'st', 'ci', 'u', 'ow', 'mb', 'gr', 'uid', 'ac', 'tg', 'k'`). Add `'m'`, and a new field `'gl'` (linked Google sub — see part 3) in the same edit:
```ts
const KEYWORD_KEYS = [
	's', 't', 'r', 'c', 'f', 'co', 'st', 'ci', 'u', 'ow', 'mb', 'gr', 'uid', 'ac', 'tg', 'k', 'm', 'gl'
] as const;
```
`ensure()`/`provision()` (qdrant.ts:103-128, unchanged) already lazily creates any payload index that's missing on next use — no migration needed, existing users' already-stored `m` values become searchable the moment this index exists.

**2. `gl` field.** Add to `src/lib/types.ts`'s `User` interface, after the `o?` line touched in [[add_device_cookie_and_session_plumbing]]: `gl?: string; // linked google sub — set when a device/local account later links Google, distinct from g (fixed at creation, used to derive id)`.

**3. Lookup helpers.** Add to `src/lib/server/user.ts`, after `is_device_only`:
```ts
export async function find_user_by_email(env: QEnv, email: string): Promise<User | null> {
	await ensure(env);
	const { scroll, f, eq } = await import('./qdrant');
	const pts = await scroll(env, f(eq('s', 'u'), eq('m', email)), 1);
	return (pts[0]?.payload as unknown as User) ?? null;
}

export async function find_user_by_google_sub(env: QEnv, sub: string): Promise<User & { id: string } | null> {
	await ensure(env);
	const { scroll, f, f_or, eq } = await import('./qdrant');
	const pts = await scroll(env, f_or([eq('s', 'u')], [eq('g', sub), eq('gl', sub)]), 1);
	const p = pts[0];
	return p ? { ...(p.payload as unknown as User), id: String(p.id) } : null;
}
```
(dynamic `import('./qdrant')` here only to avoid widening this file's static import list for two functions that need `scroll`/`f`/`f_or`/`eq` alongside the already-statically-imported `ensure`/`upsert`/etc — check whether those four are already statically imported at the top of user.ts before adding a dynamic import; if not already present, prefer adding them to the existing static `import { ensure, upsert, retrieve_one, retrieve_many, uuid_from, ZV, V, type QEnv } from './qdrant';` line instead, matching this file's existing style — the dynamic-import form above is only a fallback if a circular-import issue makes the static form fail, which is unlikely here since qdrant.ts doesn't import from user.ts).

`find_user_by_google_sub` checks both `g` (a pure fresh Google signup's id-anchor) and `gl` (a device/local account that later linked Google) in one filter — a future Google login only ever needs to call this once, regardless of which of the two histories the account has.

**4. Fix password verification at the root, not per-caller.** `verify_user_pw` (user.ts:87-95) currently only ever checks `uuid_from(email)` directly, which misses any account whose real id came from somewhere else (a device account that later set a password). Rewrite it to try the indexed lookup first, falling back to the legacy derived-id path (covers accounts created before this change with zero migration):
```ts
export async function verify_user_pw(env: QEnv, email: string, password: string): Promise<(User & { id: string }) | null> {
	const found = await find_user_by_email(env, email);
	const id = found ? undefined : await uuid_from(email);
	const u = found ?? (id ? await get_user(env, id) : null);
	if (!u || u.o !== 'local' || !u.h) return null;
	const uid = found ? (await find_user_by_email(env, email))!.id ?? id! : id!;
	return (await verify_pw(password, u.h)) ? { ...u, id: uid } : null;
}
```
On reflection, simplify — the above re-derives `uid` awkwardly; do this instead, computing the id once:
```ts
export async function verify_user_pw(env: QEnv, email: string, password: string): Promise<(User & { id: string }) | null> {
	await ensure(env);
	const { scroll, f, eq } = await import('./qdrant');
	const pts = await scroll(env, f(eq('s', 'u'), eq('m', email)), 1);
	const hit = pts[0];
	const id = hit ? String(hit.id) : await uuid_from(email);
	const u = (hit?.payload as unknown as User) ?? (await get_user(env, id));
	if (!u || u.o !== 'local' || !u.h) return null;
	return (await verify_pw(password, u.h)) ? { ...u, id } : null;
}
```
Callers change from `verify_user_pw(...)` returning `User | null` to returning `(User & { id: string }) | null` — check `src/routes/api/auth/login/+server.ts` (full file, already read): it currently separately recomputes `id: await uuid_from(e)` (line 18) when building the session — that recomputation is now WRONG for a linked-via-device account (same bug this whole step exists to fix, just at the call site instead of inside `verify_user_pw`). Fix login/+server.ts:
```ts
export const POST: RequestHandler = async ({ request, cookies, locals }) => {
	if (locals.user) return json({ ok: true });
	const body = (await request.json().catch(() => null)) as { e?: string; p?: string };
	const e = body?.e?.trim().toLowerCase();
	const p = body?.p ?? '';
	if (!e || !p) throw error(400, 'email and password required');
	const u = await verify_user_pw(env, e, p);
	if (!u) throw error(401, 'invalid credentials');
	const session = await encode_session(env.SECRET, { id: u.id, username: u.u, picture: u.p, email: u.m });
	cookies.set('session', session, { path: '/', httpOnly: true, maxAge: 604800, sameSite: 'lax' });
	return json({ ok: true });
};
```
(only the `id: u.id` on the `encode_session` call changes, from the file's previous separate `id: await uuid_from(e)`.)

**5. New endpoint: attach a password to the current session's user, in place.** Create `src/routes/api/auth/set-password/+server.ts` (new file) — deliberately NOT a reuse of `register`'s `create_pw_user` (which always computes `id = uuid_from(email)` and would create a second, disconnected record):
```ts
import { json, error } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import type { RequestHandler } from './$types';
import { env } from '$env/dynamic/private';
import { find_user_by_email, patch_user } from '$lib/server/user';
import { uuid_from } from '$lib/server/qdrant';
import { hash_pw } from '$lib/server/pw';
import { encode_session } from '$lib/server/session';

export const POST: RequestHandler = async ({ request, locals, cookies }) => {
	if (!locals.user) throw error(401, 'auth');
	const body = (await request.json().catch(() => null)) as { email?: string; password?: string };
	const email = body?.email?.trim().toLowerCase();
	const password = body?.password ?? '';
	if (!email || password.length < 6) throw error(400, 'email + password(>=6) required');

	const by_index = await find_user_by_email(env, email);
	const legacy_id = await uuid_from(email);
	const claimed_by_someone_else =
		(by_index && by_index.g !== locals.user.id && (by_index as { id?: string }).id !== locals.user.id) ||
		(!by_index && legacy_id !== locals.user.id && (await find_user_by_email(env, email)) === null
			? false
			: false);
	// simplified: the only real conflict is an existing record at a DIFFERENT id than ours
	const existing_id = (by_index as { id?: string })?.id;
	if (existing_id && existing_id !== locals.user.id) throw error(409, 'email already in use');

	const updated = await patch_user(env, locals.user.id, { m: email, h: await hash_pw(password), o: 'local' });
	if (!updated) throw error(404, 'account not found');

	const session = await encode_session(env.SECRET, { id: locals.user.id, username: updated.u, email, is_device: false });
	cookies.set('session', session, { path: '/', httpOnly: true, maxAge: 604800, sameSite: 'lax' });
	return json({ ok: true });
};
```
The conflict check went through an awkward draft above — the actual conflict logic is only the final `if (existing_id && existing_id !== locals.user.id)` line; delete the unused `claimed_by_someone_else`/`legacy_id` dead-end lines when implementing, they were left in to show the reasoning trail, not as code to ship — the real, minimal check is: does `find_user_by_email` already resolve to some OTHER id? If so, reject; otherwise it's either free or already ours, and `patch_user` (already exists, `user.ts:49-67`, merges onto the current session's own record) is always correct to call. `patch_user` preserves the account's existing embedding vector (per its own doc comment) and merges `m`/`h`/`o` onto the SAME `id` — no new record, no fork.

**6. Google linking.** `src/routes/google/+server.ts` (full file, 89 lines, already read) currently bounces any logged-in visitor immediately (line 19: `if (locals.user) throw redirect(302, '/app');`) — meaning a device user can never even reach the OAuth flow to link. Change the callback branch (lines 25-63) to detect an in-progress link vs. a fresh login/signup, and change the early bounce so only a *linked* (non-device) session still gets redirected away (a fully set-up user clicking a stray `/google` link has nothing to do there, same as today) while a device-only session is allowed through to link:
```ts
export const GET: RequestHandler = async ({ url, cookies, locals }) => {
	if (locals.user && !locals.user.is_device) throw redirect(302, '/app');

	const code = url.searchParams.get('code');
	const state = url.searchParams.get('state');

	if (code) {
		const stored_state = cookies.get('oauth_state') ?? null;
		const stored_verifier = cookies.get('oauth_verifier') ?? null;
		if (!state || !stored_state || !stored_verifier || state !== stored_state) throw error(400, 'bad_oauth');
		const g = await google_client(url.origin);
		let tokens: { accessToken(): string };
		try {
			tokens = await g.validateAuthorizationCode(code, stored_verifier);
		} catch {
			throw error(400, 'oauth_failed');
		}
		const ures = await fetch('https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/userinfo', {
			headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${tokens.accessToken()}` }
		});
		if (!ures.ok) throw error(400, 'userinfo_failed');
		const gu = (await ures.json()) as { sub: string; picture?: string; email?: string };
		if (!gu.email) throw error(400, 'email_required');

		let id: string;
		let username: string;
		if (locals.user?.is_device) {
			// linking an existing device account — attach the google sub, keep the same id
			const updated = await patch_user(env, locals.user.id, { gl: gu.sub, p: gu.picture, m: gu.email, o: 'google' });
			if (!updated) throw error(404, 'account not found');
			id = locals.user.id;
			username = updated.u;
		} else {
			// fresh login/signup — try an already-linked account first, then the normal derived-id path
			const linked = await find_user_by_google_sub(env, gu.sub);
			const id_preview = linked ? linked.id : await uuid_from(gu.sub);
			const existed = linked ? true : !!(await get_user(env, id_preview));
			id = linked ? linked.id : await save_user(env, gu.sub, gu.picture, gu.email, 'google');
			username = linked ? linked.u : gu.email.split('@')[0].toLowerCase();

			const ref = (cookies.get('ref_code') ?? '').trim();
			if (!existed && ref) await attribute_referral(env, id, ref);
			await ensure_partner_code(env, id);
			cookies.delete('ref_code', { path: '/' });
		}

		const session = await encode_session(env.SECRET, { id, username, picture: gu.picture, email: gu.email, is_device: false });
		cookies.set('session', session, { path: '/', httpOnly: true, maxAge: 604800, sameSite: 'lax' });
		cookies.delete('oauth_state', { path: '/' });
		cookies.delete('oauth_verifier', { path: '/' });
		throw redirect(302, '/app');
	}
	// ...start leg (lines 65-87) unchanged
};
```
Add `patch_user`, `find_user_by_google_sub` to this file's existing `import { save_user, get_user } from '$lib/server/user';` line. The referral-attribution block (`attribute_referral`/`ensure_partner_code`) deliberately only runs in the fresh-login branch, not the link branch — a device account linking Google isn't a new signup, it already existed, so it shouldn't be newly attributed to a referral code (matches this file's own existing `!existed` guard's intent, just made explicit for the link case by skipping the block entirely rather than needing a new condition).

**7. Register collision guard.** `src/routes/api/auth/register/+server.ts` (full file, already read) currently always succeeds at `uuid_from(email)`, silently overwriting/merging onto whatever's there — including, after this step, an email that's already claimed via `set-password` by some OTHER (unrelated, not-currently-logged-in) device account. Add the same conflict check used in `set-password`, right after computing `id`:
